  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given, $I=\\int_{0}^{2} e^{x^{4}}(x-\\alpha) d x=0$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\int_{0}^{2} e^{x^{4}} x d x=\\int_{0}^{2} e^{x^{4}} \\alpha d x$\r\nHere, we see that $\\int_{0}^{2} e^{x^{4}} x d x$ gives us an area between two curves $e^{x^{4}}$ and $x$ from $x=0$ to $x=2$\r\nSimilarly. $\\int_{0}^{2} e^{x^{4}} \\alpha d x$ gives us a same area, so $a$ should lies between 0 to $2, i . e ., \\alpha \\in(0,2)$",
    "image": null
  },
